WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.603
When it goes wrong: AI-specific incidents.

2
00:00:02.883 --> 00:00:07.213
Your incident process can borrow almost everything from SRE practice.

3
00:00:07.493 --> 00:00:23.557
Google’s workbook describes incident command roles (an incident commander who coordinates and holds any role not delegated, a communications lead who keeps stakeholders updated, and an operations lead who applies the fixes), and two rules worth memorizing:

4
00:00:23.837 --> 00:00:32.669
declare early (a managed incident resolves faster than an unmanaged one) and mitigate first (stop the impact before you understand the cause).

5
00:00:32.949 --> 00:00:40.629
Once several people are involved, keep a living incident document with the timeline, working theories, and what has been ruled out.

6
00:00:40.909 --> 00:00:46.861
What’s different for LLM systems is how incidents are detected and what mitigation looks like.

7
00:00:47.141 --> 00:00:53.584
Semantic failures don’t raise errors, so they’re often found by users, support staff, or social media.

8
00:00:53.864 --> 00:01:01.651
Refusals return HTTP 200, so error-rate alerts miss refusal spikes; track refusal rate on its own.

9
00:01:01.931 --> 00:01:11.317
And the causes include things outside your code: a provider model update, a change in the traffic mix, a newly indexed document, a new attack technique.

10
00:01:11.597 --> 00:01:14.349
Five failure types cover most AI incidents.

11
00:01:14.629 --> 00:01:18.064
For each, have the mitigation built and tested before launch.
